perf notes -- mma.m16n8k16 assignment path
==========================================
Goal: get the fp16 D=128 iteration under 7 ms (the original Triton kernel
runs at 7.7 ms/iteration on the same shapes).

Design (src/assign_mma.cuh), block = 64 points (4 warps x 16 rows):
  1. A operands live in registers for the whole K loop: each lane loads its
     8 halves per 16-wide d-step once (a[DS][4] u32), so x is never re-read.
  2. Centroid tiles (BK=32) are copied into padded shared memory with
     cp.async and double buffered; the padding (row stride D+8 halves) is
     what makes the per-warp B-fragment access bank-conflict free.
  3. One __syncthreads per tile: the prefetch for tile i+1 is issued right
     after the barrier, into the buffer consumed two tiles earlier.
  4. mma.sync.aligned.m16n8k16.row.col.f32.f16/bf16 per (d-step, n-tile);
     A fragments stay in registers, B fragments are 2 half2 loads.
  5. The epilogue never touches shared memory: each lane derives distances
     from its own accumulator registers (csq - 2*dot suffices, x^2 is
     constant per row), picks the local best over its 2 columns and reduces
     across the 4-lane row group with 2 xor-shuffles; the running best and
     index stay in registers, only lane t==0 stores the final label.

Dispatch: mma (D in {32,64,128,256,512}, fp16/bf16, sm_80+) -> WMMA -> SIMT.
FK_DISABLE_MMA=1 / FK_DISABLE_WMMA=1 force the fallbacks.

Measured (RTX 5090, B=32 N=74256 K=1000, fp16):
  D=32  2.8 ms  54 TFLOP/s      D=256  9.0 ms 136 TFLOP/s
  D=64  3.3 ms  93 TFLOP/s      D=512 21.8 ms 112 TFLOP/s
  D=128 4.7 ms 129 TFLOP/s
  batch fp16 D=128: 4.6 ms/iteration (130 TFLOP/s) vs Triton 7.7 ms (79).
Before this change the same shapes ran at 39/39/35/11 TFLOP/s
(D=128/256/512: WMMA/SIMT).

Validation: full suite 89 passed / 3 skipped, including new fp16/bf16
dimension coverage for the mma path and both FK_DISABLE_* fallback runs;
assignment results match the pure-torch reference after one iteration.
Known gap: D>512 still falls back to the generic kernels (~0.6 TFLOP/s at
D=1024); streaming the A operand with ldmatrix is the next step.
